I hate to say this, but this is indicative of a larger problem with
how DEC has handled the Univ. of Wisconsin-Madison sales.

I am a computer systems manager of a medium-large VMScluster for a
group of Univ. of Wisconsin physicists stationed at CERN in Geneva,
Switzerland.  The physicists I work for are from the same Univ. of
Wisconsin - Madison department that John Beacom is from, the physics
department.  I have been at this job for 3.5 years now.

We used to have a dedicated DEC salesperson handling the UWisc-Madison
account (call him Mr. A).  He was competent, professional, and fast
whenever we had questions, problems, or price quote requests.  About a
year ago, Mr. A took a different assignment in DEC, and the
UWisc-Madison sales were handed off to a different DEC employee (call
him Mr. X).

Mr. X lasted three months.  Then came another salesman (Mr. Y) who
seemed to last only two months.  Mr. Y was followed by yet another
salesman (Mr. Z) who lasted three months.  Mr. Z has been replaced by
DECdirect, which is now able to quote educational discount prices for
UWisc-Madison.

In short: within a one-year period, the UWisc-Madison had >five<
different contacts for sales and orders.

The worst part of this whole affair is that only two transitions were
handled well.  In the other two, THERE WAS NO INDICATION that the
saleperson had left the job.  I lost weeks on important price quote
queries while my email disappeared into the black hole that was the
former salesperson's contact point.  And when I finally found out who
the new salesperson was, I had to go through a whole new round of
explaining our needs and what price quotes we needed.

No wonder the UWisc-Madison DEC sales seems like a mess.  It certainly
WAS a mess for a year and there are probably a lot of people with 
numbers of old DEC contacts written on post-it notes who have never
been told that the DEC sales staff was changed (four times).

There are certainly signs that this situation is getting better.
DECdirect has been in place since around August 1994, and seems to be
stable.  One new DEC facility that has helped me >immensely< is
order.sales.digital.com.  I can log on and get price quotes (for
normal U.S. prices) instantly, which lets me shop and compare >before<
I need to actually have a price quote generated.  Although it has its
glitches, order.sales.digital.com is one of the most useful tools DEC
offers for sales support.

Steve, I don't want to rag on in a reply to one of your messages.  To
me, you seem to be one of the most responsible (and well-tempered) DEC
employee out there.  Your contribution in the various DEC newsgroups
is much appreciated, as is the growing presence on the net of DEC
employees from many places within the company.

But DEC sales staff for UWisc-Madison for the last year has been a
real nightmare.  I really wish my story above was a fabrication, but
unfortunately it is the truth (and I can certainly provide the names
of A, X, Y, and Z to prove this).

I gather that DEC's new advertising campaign runs along the line of
"whatever it takes".  I hope this applies to customer relations as
well (including educational facilities).  To put my money where my
mouth is, I would >welcome< some sort of contact with DEC sales to let
them know what I (as a customer) need.  I have field-tested DEC
hardware and software, and I would love to field-test good customer
relations as well.  If you would like to put me in touch with somebody
who wants to hear what is good and bad about DEC sales support, I
would be happy to offer my views.
