If you want DEC equipment, buy DEC equipment.  However, don't buy anything
straight from DEC, buy it from a reseller.  Basically, that's the way DEC
wants all non-mongo-huge accounts to go these days. We bought a machine
that way and it was actually quite easy. Call up a few resellers (see the
DEC trade rags for some names) get quotes, and let it be known that you're
shopping around for a reseller.  They'll fight each other for the sale and
you'll get a much better price than DEC lists.  Do note that the one
reseller that DEC explicitly said was taking over for the local sales
office had the worst prices and the slowest response.  Guess they thought
they were inheriting a monopoly.  SHOP AROUND!  The best price is only a
phone call away. 

Caveat - if you want the educational discount price you'll probably still
have to buy it from DEC, at least, as of a few months ago.   I've never
tried ordering anything as large as a system through DEC direct, but it
should work. Just have your academic discount agreement number and a PO
handy. (On the other hand, still check the resellers - the academic
discounts aren't all that huge on hardware, and the resellers might
actually beat it.)

The one problem we had buying from a reseller was getting the ownership
transferred from them to us, which took a month.  Yes, it was DEC screwing
up again.  It only took 15 calls to DEC by each of us to get it worked out.
Hint - the (new) policy is that the reseller has to send the paperwork to
a site OTHER than the local sales office.  Second hint, keep checking on 
the paperwork because it may vaporize even at the designated site.

I agree that it takes a new mind set, but once one accepts the idea that
there is no "local sales office"  (except for companies the size of GE) and
that mere mortals have to deal with DEC direct or resellers, it actually
isn't such a terrible sales model.  DEC support is still by DEC, and it has
actually been pretty good, at least with the assorted minor hardware and
software problems I've run into on the new system. 

Good luck,

David Mathog
