FLAME ON

One of these ads ran last night in the middle of "Father of the Bride." 
This was the "generation gap" ad that finishes with a misty 3 second 60's
flashback.  Somebody else in this string described the ad as a bad acid 
flashback, not having had one, I can't say for sure, but it sure fit what I 
thought one would be like!

Besides, after watching it, I found myself wondering what the purpose of
the ad was. 

Certainly the point wasn't to get Joe Q. Citizen to jump up off his sofa
and dash down to the nearest CompUsa and plunk down his $$$.  To begin
with, only those who already knew that Alpha existed would have had any
idea what the ad was about.  If old Joe had blinked for about 1/2 a second
he would never have even figured out that computers were involved. (Hint,
it helps to show computers in computer ads, over and over again,...).  Ok,
so Joe is Sherlock Holmes incarnate, figures out that this is the hottest
machine on the planet, and tries to buy one.  He *does* go down to CompUsa,
can't find any Alphas, and leaves with a PowerMac.  If he's really
persistent, figures out how to contact Digital (also not in the ad, even in
small print) he finds out that there are no Alpha's appropriate/affordable
for his home or small business, and he *still* buys a PowerMac.  If Joe is
really, really, really, persistent he actually arranges to test drive a WNT
Alpha (not having a clue what VMS or OSF/1 is, and not wanting to pay an
extra 3K$ to find out), but he finds that while it runs his existing
Windows software, it runs slower than his current machine which cost 1/5
as much.  He compares the prices to upgrade to native software, and he
*still* buys a PowerMac.   I can't think of *any* scenario where this ad
results in Joe buying an Alpha. 

On the other hand, MIS directors are notoriously susceptible to phsychelic
advertising.  This Monday the orders for AXP 7000's must be just poring in.

However, all is not lost, maybe a few doped up Unix programmers in the
audience may have been motivated by the tone of these ads to at least take
a look at an Alpha. 

Seriously, it's hard to see how this ad campaign is going to result in any
sales whatsoever. 

The only reasonable hypothesis is that these ads are being shown solely to
generate some name recognition. After all, the word "DIGITAL" is on the
screen for about two seconds, and is even legible once it stops jumping
around and distorting. Of course, since there are no PRODUCTS in the ad the
only thing that most people are likely to associate with them is a migraine
brought on by the jarring music and flashing images.  

It would be nice to think that all of this sound and fury was a well
planned advertising blitz leading up to something useful, like the
introduction of an AXP priced for the masses, one that would actually run 
the software that people use (ie, word processors, spreadsheets, personal
databases).  You know, like an Alpha equivalent of the PowerMac.  I'm not
holding my breath.  (If the Multia really fits this bill then maybe they 
should be advertising that instead of going on about generation gaps.)

Please correct me if I'm wrong, but there is NO POINT generating mass name
recognition unless you've got something to sell to the masses!  

Maybe DEC's TV advertising should be reserved for it's printers and PCs
instead of the Alpha. That way there might be some chance of actually
generating sales based on the advertising,  since the products in question
would then be things that the TV viewers actually buy and that DEC can
actually sell them. 

By the way, I got the chance to play with a 200 4/166 the other day.  Of 
course, there wasn't any software on it that would motivate me to buy it.
No native word processor, spread sheet or anything useful like that. (Not
even DECwrite, or at least I couldn't find it)  It wasn't networked so I
couldn't test their OpenGL compatibility with SGI's current GL (one of the 
few possible motivations for me to buy one). The new desktop was installed,
short impression: nobody's going to be giving up their Mac or PC for that.
The machine did have softwindows loaded, albeit with no real applications
installed. So I tried the paint tool and the text editing tool. Both were
much slower than my home 486 DX66. In fact, at my typical typing speed
(around 60-80 words per minute) 30 characters into the line I was about 8
characters out in front of the text tool.  This is worse than annoying -
since moving from typewriters my typing style has become "go flat out and
smack the delete key quickly on any typos". Well, I can't type that way on a
system that is 8 characters behind me!  List for this machine is around
$10K.  My conclustion: a $4500 PowerMac (loaded) would be a better
productivity tool for anybody not doing *really* serious number crunching
or graphics.  Ok, people do buy workstations explicitly for the number
crunching and graphics.  Still, a PowerMac crunches nearly as fast and it's 
getting faster, its graphics are good and getting better, and it still does
all of the productivity things that a user could want.  (Pentium too,
floating divide glitch not withstanding.) 

Digital still doesn't have a mass market Alpha product.  Apparently, they
never will. Seems a bit silly to be spending serious advertising money to
be sure that even the unwashed masses know it. 

FLAME OFF

David Mathog
