tony@newcastle.edu.au (Tony Dyson) wrote:

> Well here's _my_ situation: I'm running some baby rs6k's at present,
> and have some money in the bank to buy a new workstation. I can
> afford either an Alpha 200 4/233 or an IBM rs6k 37T. Looking at the
> SPEC and Linpack numbers the Alpha wins hands down. _However_ we
> spend most of our time running Gaussian, which doesn't behave like
> a SPEC benchmark. Our local DEC reseller has given us the smaller
> 4/166 to play with for a few days, and judging on its performance I
> estimate the 370 will kick the Alpha 4/233's butt running Gaussian.
>
> WHY? I have no idea. But you lot can come up with all the performance
> numbers in the world, and from here it looks like they mean diddly
> squat. I really do like the idea of the new Alpha workstations... but
> why is an Alpha with SPECfp 184 whipped by an RS6k with SPECfp 121?
> Surely the DEC design gurus didn't burden their latest blazing-fast
> CPU with a lousy memory bus??

No, they didn't.  And this is a perfect example of why it is
>imperative<, if you really care at all about the performance of a
specific application (in your case, Gaussian codes), that you benchmark
exactly that application on exactly the system you're gonna buy.  There
is >no other way< to know how it will run.  Exatrapolations work
sometimes--maybe even often if you're careful--but they are bound to get
you into trouble sooner or later.

While I don't have nearly enough info to tell why your particular codes
are running so much better on the RS6K, I'm not at all surprised.  RS6Ks
have many features from the memory design, to the instruction set, to
the compliers, lovingly tuned to run floating point codes fast.


