Ok, since I've been contradicted by conjecture, here are the facts:

From my experience, 10 minutes is way out of line, even with just 6 MB.  In
fact, my 2000 used to have just 6 MB, and it was not any slower to log into;
just easier to bog down.

My 2000 takes about 30 seconds to get the session manager, and another 10 to 15
to get a DECterm.  I figure that's not too bad for a machine that only has
about .9 VUPs (if that) and uses a lot of programmed I/O.  I don't run Motif.

As I write this, SHOW MEMORY shows that I've got only 15895 pages in use.
That's with one Session Manager open, three DECterms, one FileView, and one
Xload.

The 2000 isn't actually "maxed out" at 14 MB.  DEC only supported 14 MB, but
the architectural limit of the 2000 was 16 MB.  I've got a third party board in
mine, which gives me all 16 MB (32768 pages).

