Hey from Georgia, y'all.  Listen, I really hate to clog the net with
such a simple, newbie question, but a few really good success stories
would help get the week off to a great start.

Here's the deal.  We've traditionally been an IBM shop.  I've been asked
to put together a proposal for a Unix-based server.  Based on my
assessment, I've recommended a DEC 2000 Model 500 Super Server with
128 megs of RAM and 10 gigs of disk running OSF/1.  It appears to me
that this machine gives us the most bang for the buck.  We'll be
running just a few apps on the machine--SAS, possibly a Gopher/HTTP
server--with no more than 10-15 simultaneous users tops.  However, the
few users who will be connected will probably be making some realtively
serious demands on the machine.

