(Simon Hackett) writes:

> One option is NTP, a pretty standard way to do distributed time
> synchronization on TCP/IP based systems...
> ...and it's a standard, widely available protocol, unlike whatever DTSS uses.

As an aside, RFC 1305 (NTP V3) has a couple of paragraphs about DTSS under
"Related Technology", with a credit for some features which were adopted into
NTP, and compares the services thus: "The DTS design places heavy emphasis on
configuration management and correctness principles when operated in a managed
LAN or LAN-cluster environment, while NTP places heavy emphasis on the accuracy
and stability of the service operated in an unmanaged, global-internet
environment." The RFC and its predecessor (1119) are IMHO worth reading even if
you're not a time service buff: they include some nice pieces of dry wit.

But to go back to the original question at the top of the thread, if it's
important that your system time tracks DST changes as they occur and you're not
already running a package that does this, then it's probably overkill to
install DECnet/OSI or Multinet or whatever just to avoid getting out of bed in
the early hours of the morning a couple of times a year. A few lines of DCL to
get the system time, add or subtract an hour, and set the result, submitted to
run at the right time of day, should suffice.

-- 
