> 
> WHY? I have no idea. But you lot can come up with all the performance
> numbers in the world, and from here it looks like they mean diddly
> squat. I really do like the idea of the new Alpha workstations... but
> why is an Alpha with SPECfp 184 whipped by an RS6k with SPECfp 121?
> Surely the DEC design gurus didn't burden their latest blazing-fast
> CPU with a lousy memory bus??

As I said in a previous message, the AlphaStations only have 512k of cache. 
Not much, by AXP standards. How much does the RS/6000 have? Also, you should
note that the 166 and the 233 don't use the same generation of AXP processors.
The 166 has a 2x8k on-chip cache whereas the 233 has 2x16k. Clock rates being
equal, the newer generation is faster because of the larger on-chip cache
(which works at the chip's speed and isn't burdened by any bus) and other
miscellaneous design improvements. At any rate, I doubt this has anything to do
with bus speeds. The memory in an AlphaStation can't meet the speed of a PCI
bus, so you could upgrade to a 1G bus and you'd still get the same speed to the
memory banks. What really matters is the size of the cache, its speed, its
design, and how it is connected to the processor. Unless of course the RS/6000
uses faster memory, but faster memory costs more and I doubt they'd do that for
a $12k system. Finally, some of the benchmarks are known to be overtaken by
technology as the entire problem (code + data) fits in current cache sizes.
This means the benchmark isn't exercising the memory at all, just the processor
and the cache. If your real application fits in the cache, great, the benchmark
is representative. If not, the benchmark doesn't tell you much. Similarly, if
your application fits in the cache on machine X but not in machine Y, X will
have a big advantage over Y. And it's perfectly ok as long as you've
benchmarked your actual application - all that matters to do is that X is
faster.

  Eric


