> : Does anybody know what the memory bandwidth is for the Alpha 200 4/233?
> : Even more to the point, is anybody running Gaussian or anything similar
> : on one of these new Alphas under OSF? I wish I could get past all the
> : marketing hype and find out something useful - the IBM rep started out
> : talking about memory bandwidth, then mentioned the number 0.5 Gb/sec, 
> : then said that the PCI bus in the Alpha had a max bandwidth of 132Mb/sec.
> : Huh? Surely those facts are not really connected?

Well, the AlphaStation is basically a high-end PC with an AXP processor. It
uses standard PC SIMMs, or so DEC claims, and consequently it will have the
memory bandwidth of a high-end PC. PCI is indeed limited to 132M/sec, which is
33MHz x 32 bits. I don't know for a fact whether this is the case on an
AlphaStation, but on a normal PC the (external) processor bus runs at 33MHz as
well. So, your main bus bandwidth is indeed on the same order as PCI, assuming
a 32 bit path.

But it's not like the salesmen's "memory bandwidth" (really the bus speed) will
determine your execution speed. The reason processors have caches is that the
memory chips can't handle the processor's speed. If your application is memory
intensive, the cache design and the performance of the memory chips will be
more important than the speed of the bus that connects the processor to the
memory banks (unless of course that bus is the weakest element in the chain,
like with the 300LX, but this isn't the case here). So the salesmen should be
telling you about the size of the internal and external caches (2x16k and 512k
for the 4/233), telling you how fast that cache is, and generally hyping about
its design. You're just not going to get anywhere near 500M/sec from the SIMMs
on an entry level workstation, even if the bus runs at that speed. But it is
indeed quite possible that the external cache is bigger and/or faster on the
RS/6000.

To take a concrete example, compare the 4/166 to the 3000-600. The former does
134.8 SPECfp at 166MHz with 2x8k and 512k cache, and (presumably) a 132M/sec
bus. If you extrapolate linearly to a 175MHz chip, you would find 142.1 SPECfp.
The 3000-600 does 162.1 in fact, even though (DEC Sweden's comparison matrix
dixit) its bus only runs at 100M/sec. The difference is due to the larger
cache. For SPECint, the difference is 113.5 extrapolated vs 114.1 actual;
presumably the integer tests use up less memory. At any rate, the only way to
know for sure it is to try the code out.

  Eric


