In article <CzoBF7.80y@info.bris.ac.uk>,
Tim Llewellyn <Tim.Llewellyn@bristol.ac.uk> wrote:
>Sorry I am confused. Do I issue the rsh command from the host that has
>the advfs file system or the host that has the tape drive? Is the advfs
>file system nfs mounted on the system with the tape drive?

I run vdump on the machine that has the filesystem, and pipes the
output to a rsh that runs a dd on the machine with the tape drive.

One example: Backing up ernst2:/usr (which is an AdvFS fileset) on a
tapestation on ernie.

ernst2# vdump -0 -b 64 -f - /usr | rsh ernie dd of=/dev/nrmt0h bs=64k

The two main thing to remember is that root must be allowed to do a
rsh to the target machine, and that the block-sizes of dd and vdump
should match (otherwise you can't restore it without using dd).

The vdump blocksize is set by the -b parameter (in kb). The
documentation of DEC OSF/1 2.x and 3.0 claims that the default is
60. Both are wrong, since the default in 2.x is 10kb, and the default
in 3.0 is 64 kb!


