Terry C Shannon writes:

>Adding further confusion to this issue is SARAH, a compound document 
>editor that DEC developed but never marketed.  Back in my days as Charlie 
>Matco, a DECcie sent me a copy of the SARAH internal release notes.  This 
>was circa 1986 or 1987 if I remember correctly.  Why DEC jilted SARAH in 
>favor of Son of Framemaker remains known only to DEC.

  SARAH/GPU/XYZ was all intended to be a desktop publisher built from
a TPU base and 'simply' extended to permit multiple fonts, embedded
pictures, etc.  Unfortunately, SARAH got way behind on its promised
deadlines, had problems with reliability (I can remember trying to run
early releases) - and then was subject to Mythical Man Month
treatment.  They staffed up at the end to bring down the elapsed time
to develop - and thereby doomed the project.

  As a result, DEC upper management was very gun shy of having DEC
build anything from scratch in the publishing arena.  Instead, they
entered into an agreement with Framemaker to provide the sources to
the original Framemaker product to DEC.  Charles Corfield, a
Framemaker founder and original author, personally gave a one-week
seminar (in a kilt, no less) to the original DECwrite development team
to let us know how the thing worked.  Purchasing the sources gave us a
working product from day one.  That made management much happier.

  [Except for being in on Charles' course, everything above is hearsay
from SARAH and senior DECwrite project members]

  From there, we started slicing and dicing the thing.  Gene Morgan
gutted the file machinery and replaced it with DDIF stuff (which was
being developed and refined at the same time), Seth Cohen (technical
lead) heavily reworked the formatting engine and oversaw the whole
beast.  Tom Dahl constructed the user interface and the internal
3-stage machinery for dealing with dialog boxes.  The rest of us
(about 8 engineers) worked up improvements in graphics, journalling,
automatic table of contents, styles, etc.

  There was also a team of 3 or 4 led by Paul Wong to port DECwrite to
Windows 3.1.  It took a couple more to port it to Ultrix.

  I was a grab-bag guy.  I did modifications all over the place (table
of contents, indexing, search engine speedups, image display, etc) and
was responsible for journalling going into the product.  It worked out
to be of dubious value to users due to reliability problems, but it
was invaluable as a bug-reporting tool.

  In all, there were about a dozen people working on the product, and
half of them were what DEC calls Principle Engineers - folks with ~6+
years of engineering experience.  It was headed up by a Consulting
Engineer with roughly double that time and who was promoted to Senior
Consulting Engineer after the project.  It was a good team of people
and we had a great time building the product - despite the pounding of
fists, shouting and arguments.

  Total development time was about 25-30 man years.  This doesn't
include CDA development time, Framemaker development time, or the
immense number of hours that users put in testing the product and
reporting the bugs.  If memory serves, during development of DECwrite
V1, a total of 3000 reports were recorded (including suggestions) of
which 800 were bugs which resulted in software crashes.  All were
fixed for V1 release.

  I doubt there's much of a relation between the Framemaker of today
and the DECwrite of today - despite the fact that each product added
many of the same features.  They were added by different teams and,
therefore, were added in different ways.  As evidence, just look at
the user interface and the treatment of the keyboard.  DECwrite tried
to bridge the gap between word processing and desktop publishing - and
pulled it off.

  I'd love to see DECwrite go public domain.  It would be a shame to
lose it completely, while going public domain might end up putting it
everywhere and provide us with a universal WYSIWYG publishing tool.
It would also be great for a whole bunch of engineers to see what goes
on inside a desktop publishing package like DECwrite.

JB
