               DECUS '94/ANAHEIM TO FEATURE LEADING SPEAKERS
                   FROM THROUGHOUT THE COMPUTER INDUSTRY 


SHREWSBURY, MA, November 16, 1994 -- DECUS '94/Anaheim, the premier event 
for Information Technology professionals dealing with the complexities of 
building and operating multivendor, multiplatform computing environments, 
will feature leading speakers from throughout the industry addressing a 
range of topics: from the future of IT, to drawing cartoons as an 
alternative career to systems development.

The list of featured speakers begins with William Strecker, vice president 
of Digital Equipment Corporation's Advanced Technology Group who will 
present the keynote address "The Future of Information Technology", on 
Monday, December 12. Strecker, who also serves as Digital's chief technical 
officer, will discuss how Digital is adapting to the changing landscape of 
the IT industry, how the IT paradigm has changed, and how the convergence of 
various technologies will continue to restructure the industry.

Presentations from key Digital figures continues on Tuesday, December 13 
when Don Harbert, Digital's vice president of UNIX Software Business, 
discusses "OSF Futures" during an informal reception. The DECUS '94/Anaheim 
program will also continue the popular DECUS "Digital Listens Panel" on 
Tuesday. The panel provides attendees the opportunity to ask questions of, 
and provide candid feedback to, Digital's top level managers and vice 
presidents. 

William Inmon, one of the founders of data warehousing, will share his 
unique insight on why today's corporations are drowning in data and starving 
for information; why the mess is as bad as it is; and what we can do about 
it, during "Building the Data Warehouse: A Management Perspective", on 
Tuesday, December 13.

Also on Tuesday, Rob Kolstad of Berkley Systems Development, Inc. will share 
his views on the future of the software industry during "Software What're We 
Gonna Do?", the keynote session for the event's UNIX Developer's Conference.

Tom Grundner, known to many as "The Father of Free Nets", will discuss the 
continuing grassroots movement to develop community networks to provide free 
and open access to electronic information in "Community Networks: Public 
Access to Electronic Information", on Wednesday, December 14.

Noted industry analyst, Terry Shannon, will present his views on the 
technologies, tactics, and strategies that will carry Digital throughout the 
coming decade in "Digital's Strategic Directions: An Analyst's Perspective", 
on Thursday, December 15.

Other noted DECUS alumni speakers returning for Anaheim include Digital's 
Richie Lary and popular columnist and network security expert Bill Hancock. 
Lary will share his insight on "The Future of Open Storage Buses" and will 
describe the technology behind modern disk drives in "B I T S with Richie 
Lary", both on Wednesday, December 14. Hancock will present several sessions 
during the symposium including "Dial-Up and Remote Access Networks", a 
first-hand look at implementing state-of-the-art systems, on Thursday, 
December 15.

Nationally syndicated cartoonist Scott Adams, the creator of Dilbert, closes 
out the symposium on Thursday, December 15. Adams, who has combined the 
careers of computer professional and syndicated cartoonist, will share his 
perspective on professional development options.

Scheduled for December 10-15, 1994 at the Anaheim Convention Center, 
Anaheim, California, DECUS '94/Anaheim will cover a variety of topics of 
interest to today's Information Technology professionals. Attendees will 
learn about the tools and techniques used to manage and develop 
heterogeneous platforms and will be exposed to state-of-the-practice 
solutions through a mix of offerings presented by industry leaders, product 
engineers and developers, and day-to-day product users.

For more information, or to register, contact DECUS Customer Service at 
1-800-DECUS55.

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is 
an association of Information Technology professionals interested in the 
products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and 
related vendors. The Association's purpose is to promote the unimpeded 
exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members and their 
organizations to be more successful. The Chapter provides each member with 
means to enhance their professional development, forums for technical 
training, mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy 
programs, and opportunities for informal disclosure and interaction with 
professional colleagues of like interests.

