I don't know about you, but personally I couldn't care less what clock rate the
chip runs at or how many TurboPipelines(TM) it has or whether the design is
WarpedScalar(TM) or HyperScalar(TM). What I'm interested in is the speed of the
box and the price I paid. Why don't you post a chart with the various
workstations benchmarked by CERN, and the number of CERN units per dollar? :-)

To answer your question: as I'm sure you are aware, the 300LX uses a cheap
bus/memory design, which significantly lowers the amount of SPECxxx per MHz. A
166MHz AlphaStation does 107.7 SPECint92 and 134.8 SPECfp92. A 233MHz
AlphaStation does 157.6 and 183.9 for a list price of $12,500 or so. I believe
a SS20 goes for around $15k. How much does the SS20 do? :-) See, THAT is the
kind of stuff I care about, what I get and for how much. Not whether DEC
"cheats" by using higher MHz or any similar FUD.

The entry-level market is bound to be dominated by PC systems. There are just
so many being sold that workstations can't compete in price/performance. I've
run some benchmarks on my software, and found that a P90 is slightly faster
than a SS20. I can get 4 P90s for the price of one SS20. I haven't benchmarked
on all the workstations you mentioned, but the P90 would be cheaper than them
anyway. So, even if the workstations were a bit faster, which may or may not be
