(There has been a brief similar discussion in the DECTEI news group.)

The point I made there is that if Digital has no or negative revenue
from a piece of software, then it should turn the code over to the user
community rather than sell it.  Here's why:

1.  My impression is that the small companies that have been buying this
stuff have only paid a pittance for the productivity software that Digital
has already disposed of.  Probably a similar amount could be collected by
just passing a collection plate around at the next big DECUS meeting. In
other words, DEC isn't getting any real money out of these sales.  (RDB
excepted). 

2.  This software is essentially vaporizing.  In academia we can still use
those pieces that have stayed on the CSLG, but I've not seen a single
advertisement or upgrade from any of the new owneers of this software.
There is getting to be a domino effect, for instance, DECwrite runs quite
well on an AXP, but without the CDA converter library it is basically
impossible to get anything into or out of it.  If it won't exchange
documents with common word processors it's doomed and they might as well
dispose of it too.  It can't be good for hardware sales to make it *more*
difficult to obtain software for either VMS or OSF/1. 

3.   At this point in time, and after so much downsizing, there is apparently
more Digital expertise outside of Digital than within it.  A lot of these 
outsiders (and insiders too, no doubt) would love to have a decent
wordprocessor, spread sheet, etc. on their workstations, but cannot afford
or justify the extra 2 to 4 fold increase in price over equivalent PC/Mac
software.  They are also perfectly capable of both maintaining and
improving all of the software that DEC is tossing out the window.  Turn the
code over to the user community, and VMS and OSF/1 will have excellent
*free* productivity software.  If Digital thinks that bundling Mosaic is a
good idea, how about a commercial grade DTP, spread sheet, etc.?  This
would sell hardware, Digital hardware. It would be perfectly acceptable for
Digital to enforce the restriction that this software be "free" only on
it's own hardware or others that it might specify.  Digital is not afraid
to use a bit of legal muscle, so they probably could pull it off.  Here's
the scenario: "We could buy that HP and then pay another $3K for
productivity software, or we could buy that Alphastation, load all of the
commercial grade free stuff."  Along the same lines, Digital might actually
want to allow ports to other platforms, but require some minimum price and
collect a royalty of maybe 10% or so on every copy sold. 

4.  There is a precedent of sorts.  Where would Unix be today if ATT had
kept the code firmly locked up at Bell Labs?

Shedding software to the user community could be done in a number of ways. 
For instance, Digital could sign over the DECwrite code to DECUS subject to
various and sundry legal limitations.  They might even do something useful
like buy back the CDA converter and donate it to the cause.  Whichever
method is chosen, I'm not saying that further development need be
monolithic.  For instance, one group might want to slice DECwrite down into
something a good deal lighter and faster, whereas another might want to
build it up towards more of a publishing orientation.  If programmers are 
interested in working on it, why not have both?

Why would anybody donate their time toward this?  Well for one, it would be 
fun.  For another, money could be made by offering consulting and support
services for this new software in excess of anything available on the Internet. 
Custom coding opportunities too.

The concept is innovative, forward thinking, and would probably sell
some extra hardware without incurring software development costs.  Ok,
Digital is probably too ossified to go for it, but maybe if we yell enough
somebody in there will get a clue - look what happend with BLISS! 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqvax.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 


