In article <1994Nov16.222302.16201@Princeton.EDU> Eric Prebys,
prebys@puhep1.Princeton.EDU writes:
>As I said in an earlier posting, I have been trying to use a DEC RZ55
>319 Meg SCSI drive with a macintosh Powerbook 160.  The powerbook
>correctly identifies the drive on the SCSI bus but is unable to format
>it or do anything else.
>
>It appears that the problem has to do with the fact that the DEC disk does
>not spin up automatically when the power is turned on, but rather waits
>for a SCSI command to be sent by the processor.  I assume that this is
>a simple command, but I've been unable to find any Mac SCSI programs
>(shareware anyway) with this option.  Does anyone know of a program (or
>preferably an extension or INIT) that can do this? or (this one is for the 
>DEC group), does anyone know if there is a jumper setting on the drive that 
>can override this "feature"?
>
> 
>Any advice would be appreciated.
>
>				-Eric Prebys
>				prebys@puhep1.princeton.edu

It's almost always a jumper setting on the disk drive itself.  We've had this
problem with quite a few older drives: Unix boxes typically issue a spin-up
scsi command, whereas Macs do not.  The drive hardware documentation can be
had from the mfgr, I don't have DEC's at hand.

Also, tho not an issue with a Powerbook using an external, many older 5.25"
drives have too great a current draw on spin-up to be used internally in Macs
even if they fit, for example some CDC/Seagate Wrens in MacIIFXes.  Also,
some really old ones have incompatible SCSI implementations, for example
Maxtors. Newer 3" mechs tend to have fewer of these hassles, ie they plug in
and work without futzing with jumpers etc.

Dan


