Jerry Pournelle used to say in his column in Byte magazine that "if you don't
know what you're doing, then deal with someone who does".  If your friend is
not computer literate, then he might be better off dealing with a computer
dealer that can provide supported and supportable solutions to your friend's
problems and questions.  

The VR299 might be a good deal for $5, but only if it'll work on your friend's
DOS system.  However, if he pays $5 for it, then he'll have paid $5 too much
for it, since it won't work on his DOS system.  It's a single frequency
monitor, and that frequency is not what the PC video cards run at these days. 
The monitor runs at 60Hz vertical refresh rate, vs. the 70Hz (or higher) 
refresh rate that most video cards use; uses composite sync, vs. separate sync;
etc.

Maybe buy the monitor for $5, and turn around and sell them to a dealer for
$10, and pocket the difference!

