|>:   I'm looking for a VCB03 and/or a Q-bus ethernet card for my VAXstation
|>: 3200. Anybody have either (or both) for relatively cheap? Of course, an
|>: ESDI or SCSI disk controller would also be really nice, too, if anybody
|>: had one that would fall within a "student budget"(tm).
|>
|>I have a VS II with VCB02 graphics.  What is the VCB03?  I've never run
|>a VS 3200, what is the processor in that?  For ethernet, I think you have to
|>use the DELQA adapter.  the DEQNA is not supported by new VMS systems, at
|>least.  I think I've seen them about $375 from the brokers.  Check the
|>brokers for third party ESDI and SCSI controllers.  I use a Dilog ESDI
|>controller, but they may not be in business anymore.  CMD makes both.
|>We've used the CMS SCSI controller with good results.
|>

    The VCB03 is not supported and will not work on the VAXstation
    3200 -- if memory serves, it was (only) used on the M-bus in the
    Firefox (VAXstation 3520 and VAXstation 3540) series.  (It was
    not a Q-bus board.)

    Most every VAXstation 3200 I am aware of shipped with a VCB02 --
    also known as the "GPX", "QDSS" or "Dragon" graphics controller.
    If you are acquiring a VCB02, make sure it is up-to-rev, as the
    earlier revisions were not entirely compatible with the KA650
    processor used in the VAXstation 3200.

    I wouldn't bother trying to get a VCB01 to work on the VAXstation
    3200  system -- it's more trouble than its probably worth.

    The Ethernet controllers for these systems are the DELQA.  (For
    S-box Q-bus systems, the DESQA is another option.)  Do not try
    to use the DEQNA -- even if you get it for free.

    Any SCSI magnetic disk controller would be a third-party controller,
    as the only Digital Q-bus SCSI controller, the KZQSA, does not support
    magnetic disks.

    The VAXstation 3100 series, or the VAXstation 4000 VLC or model 60
    would be a better choice for a student budget (tm).  All of these
    systems have graphics, SCSI, and ethernet capabilities.  They're
    as fast or faster than the VAXstation 3200, and they're smaller.


