The reason DEC isn't releasing the Multia for anything else is that the Multia
is not a general-purpose computer, officially, but a one stop shopping
interface to everything that happens to need a piece of hardware to run on. The
reason the Multia is cheap is that the sharks who set the guidelines for
pricing haven't been told that it CAN be used as a general purpose computer,
and have removed the $5000 internal tax on equipment which might compete with
the 3000 workstations. If they'd been told the Multia is a general purpose NT
computer that will be sold together with a license for the Multia software,
they'd have priced it $8,500.

Why DEC is putting up with that ridiculous pricing policy is beyond my
understanding, but it certainly is a business decision. DEC believes they will
make more money this way; I disagree, but I'm not the one in charge. The last
thing DEC is scared about is selling a bunch of hardware that would then run
someone else's software. DEC is giving ISVs a very nice discount, and Linus got
a free AXP. That's because DEC wants people to write code for their hardware so
that people buy it and they increase their market share.

