In article <1994Nov15.165115.1@segate.sunet.se>, eric@segate.sunet.se (Eric Thomas) writes:
> Finally, I realized how stupid I had been. The one
> thing that DEC that still works more or less is technical support. So, I called
> in and told the droid I had a problem with a "LK450-AA" and got an incident
> number. It took a while, but they called back with a competent answer. The
> answer was that DEC has not developed such a driver, and has no plans to ever
> port the 3.1 driver to NT. Then I got all sorts of competent, but alas
> disappointing, advice on how to try to make as many keys as possible work.

  The LK450 is brain-dead. It isn't actually built by DEC, but by an OEM. It
doesn't implement a LK250 compatibility mode (though it claims it does, the
ESC key is mapped wrong and keys that don't exist on the LK250 return strings
instead of being dead). It does have a number of extended modes, while the
LK250 only had LK250 and IBM modes.

  I did the LK250 support for MS-Kermit and I get lots of requests for LK450
support. My standard response is that while I have the programming information,
doing a LK450 driver would be a whole new support set, not just some minor
tweaks to the LK250 support, and I don't have the time.

  DEC *does* make the LK450 information available under non-disclosure, so you
could conceivably obtain it and a NT DDK (device driver development kit) and
write your own LK450 driver for NT. You might even be able to sell it. If you
want to pursue this, send me email and I'll give you the contact info at DEC
for obtaining the LK450 information.

