The entire reason people buy alphas (or at least why WE buy alphas) is
because they are FAST & cheap.  If I didn't care about speed I'd buy a
Sun, the reason being that if I bought a Sun, I'd be able to run just
about any unix software package the day it came out.  If a Sun and a
DEC sell for about the same amount of $$, and are roughly equivalent
in speed, the decision is a no-brainer.  99% of people will go with
the Sun.

Alphas have usually been 2x as fast (or more) than the competition for
the same amout of $$.  I'm sure their new NT only machines are at
least half-again as fast as a 300LX, and it ticks me off that they
won't sell them with OSF/1.

I imagine that they're worried that if they sold the new low-end
machines with OSF/1 or VMS, then they wouldn't be able to get rid of
the 300LXs they've got sitting around.  

With the new MIPS and Hyper(is that the name?) Sparc based machines
due out next year, their technology lead is going to disappear.  They
might be better served by getting some volume out in the market than
trying to make a large profit on each machine they sell.  For example,
a 3000LX would sell well if priced against a Sparc Classic, but if its
priced against a SS5, then its not going to do nearly as well.

