In article <3a8kkp$sgl@crl.crl.com>, jbarnes@crl.com (John Barnes) writes:
>We are migrating our software from the VAX to the ALPHA.  We've compiled
>our C code with /standard=vaxc.  When we try to run our software we try
>to run the program we get
>
>			error activating image vaxcrtl_d56_tv.exe
>			ident mismatch with shareable image.
>
>The logical vaxcrtl_tv points to vaxcrtl_d56_tv.exe.  We just tried
>reloading C put that didn't help.  This the rtl has the number 56
>it must be left over from vax vms 5.6.  Not sure why we don't have
>one numbered 6.1.  Any help on this would be greatly appreciated.

The 56 doesn't refer to a version but rather 56-bit D_floating.  That
image is a translated VAX C RTL image.  If you're compiling native using
DEC C on the Alpha you shouldn't have any references to VAXCRTL.  Are
you mistakenly including it in your link procedures?  You don't have
to do so with DEC C - the linker will find the RTL automatically.
If that's not the problem, link with /MAP/FULL/CROSS and check the map
for references to VAXCRTL, then see what you get.

I'm a bit puzzled, though, as you should get other errors if you are
inadvertently linking against the translated VAXCRTL unless you
explicitly compiled wih /TIE and linked /NONATIVE_ONLY.

