I'm very glad that Steve Lionel, Alan Rollow, Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz and
others at Digital read this newsgroup, as well as other places like the
osf managers mailing list, and are apparently encouraged to do so by the
company.  They've often provided help or insight which appears far
beyond what they are probably expected to do.

Knowing that this assistance would be available was very important 
in convincing us to go ahead and try staying with Digital when moving
beyond our DECstation products.

Over the last several months, though, there's been a definite shift
which I find disturbing.  Last year, we used to frequently see people
like Gail Grant, and AJ Casamento who seemed to be much more close
to the marketing side of Digital in these groups.  The presence
of these people has definitely faded, (and I assume it's because
some of them, at least, are no longer with Digital).

If people in Marketing or Sales are no longer paying attention to what is said
here, it is that much more difficult for people to understand what
customers want. 

It is very easy to dismiss  the proceedings in
comp.sys.dec as primarily representing the University market.  It's true
that many of us have relatively limited equipment budgets, tend to
push our equipment into regimes which are relatively unsupported, and
expect educational discounts which further erode already slim margins. 

This does not make our opinions irrelevant.  
If there is resistance to a new product, or concept 
(like Multia) within the educational sector, a company should ask itself
if they believe that an MIS department of some Fortune 500 company could
actually be more adventuresome than people in a university.

