> series boxes).  So, I guess, "mainframe" means that it runs IBM 370 code
> on a virtual IBM-370 machine inside an IBM box running some version of 
> VM-370.  Hmmm, now I see why IBM has this market tied up!

Please, let's not confuse:

1. The definition of "mainframe" used by people who've been involved with
mainframes for, say, 10 years or more, and

2. The definition of "mainframe" as the press, or various advertisement
agencies, or usenet flamers, would have it.

I've never seen a "mainframe" defined a "a box to which you can hook 400 phone
lines". That's either a "terminal controller" or maybe an "SNA controller" :-)
Likewise, the absolute amount of gigabytes you can hook hasn't really been the
factor. It's a lot simpler than this. A mainframe is a central computer on
which a bunch of users (or jobs, or transactions, whatever you're in the
business of doing) are running concurrently. Obviously the definition of "a
bunch" varies with time as technology improves. The 370 was a mainframe back
when personal computers came with 8k of memory with a 32k upgrade option for
the richest, and mainframes came with 1M. A 386 is faster, has more memory,
more I/O bandwidth, etc, but it's not from the same generation, so you can't
judge them by the same standards. The 370 had dozens of users, the 386
typically has one.

The real problem is the definition of what the mainframe does. On a traditional
mainframe, the user gets 100% of his work done on the mainframe. Concretely,
you type SHOW USER or WHO or whatever, and the list is so long that you get
bored and press Ctrl-C. A modern, high-end traditional mainframe will serve
4000-5000 users in this fashion. It will be hooked to a few thousand square
feet of disk units at 100G each, it will have some 10G of central storage plus
a similar amount of slower external storage, used for paging or what have you,
and it will be able to do 256 to 1024 disk I/O at the same time, based on how
big your disk farm really is. And the I/O will run at the same speed whether
you're doing 3 or 500 concurrently. It will have 6-10 processors, each of them
maybe twice as fast as the 3000-600 sitting on your desk, and your Alpha will
run any one I/O faster than the monster. In fact, the Alpha will be faster for
you, at least in most cases. But no matter what its CPU speed or bus speed, the
workstation isn't a mainframe. Try putting 200 users on it all working at the
same time, and you'll quickly see why. I consider the 7000, 9000 and 10000 to
be mainframes, by that definition. But it's not the CPU speed or the number of
disks, it's the ability to process many users at the same time with subsecond
response time - the ability to scale up to the load, if you want. This also
requires an operating system that scales up. To take a stupid example, with
5000 users, if the function that locates control blocks for user JOE runs
through a chain looking for the name "JOE", you lose. It will spin 2500 times
on the average and there will be 5000 people wanting to do that. The same
function would run very fast on a typical mini with 50 users. It probably
wouldn't occur to you not to use a linked list if you weren't specifically
designing for that many users.

That was the traditional mainframe. Nowadays, people are starting to use
mainframes as large servers. Users click on a PC to prepare a transaction, and
the mainframe runs it. This kind of mainframe can be replaced, load permitting,
with a smaller server (say a 2100, or even a 3000-600). This doesn't mean the
2100 or 3000-600S has become a mainframe, it means you've been able to replace
the mainframe with a smaller system, because the PC is doing part of the job
and the mainframe's job is simpler and does not require megabytes of
presentation software and context, it just looks up a database and returns the
info.

But, of course, none of this helps with advertisements. So people find one
mainframe that is still being  used and that doesn't perform quite as well as
the box they're trying to sell, and they say "Hey, my box is faster than this,
so it's got to be a mainframe!" By that same token, my dial-up phone is a
high-speed network link and the floppy on my PC is a high capacity backup unit.
But, of course, since people usually know what "high capacity backup unit"
means, they would never buy that. On the other hand, since people don't usually
know what a mainframe is supposed to be, they buy the "mainframe in a PC" ads
happily :-)

  Eric


