For a few months now I have been trying to find NT drivers for the LK450
keyboard. This is a PC keyboard which is, mechanically, identical to a VT420 or
workstation keyboard. Couple with a terminal emulator like KEAterm, it gives
you ALL the keys in a real VT terminal at the RIGHT locations. Byebye escape
and ALT sequences! No more finger cramps trying to keep one finger on both PgUp
and PgDn! Obviously, DEC marketing being what it is, this keyboard is totally
unknown and DEC does not use it on its own PCs, nor do the salesmen know about
it. But, if you belong to the select group of people who know the password,
"LK450-AA", you can have one shipped within a few business days via DEC Direct.

Unfortunately, in order to use the new keys you need an updated keyboard
driver. The keyboard comes with a diskette with a Windows 3.1 driver. I am now
using NT more and more often and need an NT driver very badly. Having half the
keys "blind" makes it impossible for me to switch to NT completely. I've tried
to get the info from DEC, but without luck. In desperation, I bought a second
keyboard, in the hope that it comes with NT drivers nowadays (I did need a
keyboard for a second PC, although it could have been a cheaper one since that
PC is seldom used). Nix. Finally, I realized how stupid I had been. The one
thing that DEC that still works more or less is technical support. So, I called
in and told the droid I had a problem with a "LK450-AA" and got an incident
number. It took a while, but they called back with a competent answer. The
answer was that DEC has not developed such a driver, and has no plans to ever
port the 3.1 driver to NT. Then I got all sorts of competent, but alas
disappointing, advice on how to try to make as many keys as possible work.

Well, the good old DEC we know and love. DEC is pushing NT. DEC sells terminals
with a certain keyboard layout. DEC provided a PC keyboard that is identical to
the one on DEC's terminals so that people who had to switch from terminal to PC
would not be inconvenienced. But, only with Windows 3.1. If you're running
Windows NT, you should not use that keyboard. But DEC still wants you to
migrate to NT, because NT is strategic. They just don't think it's strategic
enough to make it possible for you to keep using your keyboard with all the
keys. I'm sure it would take at least one day of work to port the #$%#%%$
driver. Without the driver, I have no option but to stop purchasing LK450
keyboards since they cost $100 and provide the same functionality as a normal
keyboard. I'm sure I'm not the only one in that situation. I wonder how much
money DEC is going to lose. I also wonder how many people are going to see the
lack of plans to ever port a simple keyboard driver as evidence that DEC's
official commitment to NT is not all that strategic in fact, or as evidence
that DEC is not a dependable business partner. I mean, come on, what does this
driver really do? It adds a handful of scan codes for the new keys to a table,
and it handles the COMPOSE key. That's all I can think of. That's all I use, at
least. And there isn't even a replacement, like a LK550 or whatever. The
functionality is lost. Thank you, DEC for permanently increasing my adrenaline
level every time I have to use my PC to connect to one of your machines. It
used to be so simple, and now I'm back to ALT+B and all that jazz. I will
remember.

  Eric


