In article 94Nov10084407@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov, ejh@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov (Edward Hartnett) writes:
>
> We here distribute some floating point (IEEE) data to various
> users. We generate it on a cray and various workstations, and now we
> have a user who wants to read the data from a fortran program on a
> decstation. He complains that he can't do this because of the order
> that the bytes are stored in. Is there any hope that we can help him
> solve his problem?

In the FORTRAN program, open the file as

     open(file='file.dat', convert='big_endian', form='unformatted',....)

and any big_endian integer or IEEE floats will be converted automatically
as the files are read in. If all of the unformatted files are big_endian 
you can alternatively do this at compile time as

FORTRAN/CONVERT=BIG_ENDIAN

On an AXP, if you compile the Fortran source with the flag /FLOAT=IEEE then 
everything will be done in IEEE format.

Regards,

John

p.s. there is also a /CONVERT=CRAY option which is big-endian integer and
"CRAY" floating point for REAL*8 and COMPLEX*16 (with no single precision).






