>Interesting comment, but at this time, what is the definition of a mainframe
>computer?  I think the definition is now the amount of I/O that can be 
>attached.  If you can hook up 400 phone lines, it must be a mainframe.  Uhhh,
>what about ethernet terminal servers?  That sort of breaks the above 
>question.  Well, how about disk storage?  How many disk drives can you 
>hook up?  Well, with clustering (or NFS in UNIX) you can hook lots of 
>drives up, logically.  Well, how about CPU speed?  DEC's alpha can probably
>run rings around anything with thousands of ECL chips on cards plugged into
>boards on swing-out gates, connected with miles of tri-lead (read IBM 370
>series boxes).  So, I guess, "mainframe" means that it runs IBM 370 code
>on a virtual IBM-370 machine inside an IBM box running some version of 
>VM-370.  Hmmm, now I see why IBM has this market tied up!  And, I also see
>why DEC doesn't even want to try to grab this market.  Who wants to try to
>explain to some 50-year-old Big Blue shop manager why the alpha is really
>better because it DOESN'T run IBM 370 machine code?  He won't listen!


It is hard to say.  Even when you stick to the traditional mainframe/mini/micro
divide, the lines have always been fuzzy.  I have heard people, for instance, 
say that the PDP-10/Decsystem 10/Decsystem 20 was a mainframe--since it
was used for the sort of things mainframes are (lots of interactive users,
etc).  Originally, a mini was the likes of a PDP 8, single or very few
users.  But then in the 80's vaxen and other minis started handling the
sort of task traditionally associated with mainframes.

These days its somewhat harder.  People seem to think that any computer
that is accessed remotely is a mainframe.  That means 370, alpha, sparc 1+...
I even heard someone talk about a 3B2 as a mainframe the other day!
Wasn't sure whether to laugh or cry :-(

Generally speaking, however, the best definition seems to be--"Its a mainframe
if we call it that".  That means IBM+Amdahl+Fujitsu+a couple of others,
mainly IBM, are it.  Dec makes minis and workstations.  Of course, thesedays
that is "servers" and "workstations", and mainframe is a dirty word.

