I assume you are referring to the "WANK" (Worms Against Nuclear Killer) worm
that ran around the DECnet internet hitting an uncounted, but very large number
of systems. I was the author of the first report of the details of the worm and
it did a LOT of things. It was poorly written, but at the time it showed up,
system security was weak enough for its shotgun attack to be fairly effective.
It tried username=password, a dictionary of common(?) passwords and the
username-password combinations that had been successful in previous attacks (it
tried to learn) and mailed off results of successes. Fortunately there were
enough errors that most of these things really didn't work as intended.

A modified version of the worm, often referred to as OILZ showed up soon
afterward and was far more successful at attacking systems be using DEC's
stupid use of user DECNET, password DECNET for the non-priv. DECnet account.

The code was full of small errors which limited its spread and the damage it
caused. If it had been properly written and a little more thought given to good
account/password checking, it could have been a much more devastating problem.
As it was, I'd describe it as a major annoyance.

I will also always remember that a major earthquake struck the SF area as I was
writing the report (and as the World Series Game 3 was beginning) possibly
causing the stupid error I made wrt a node name which still causes some
confusion. :-( I was writing it up when the quake struck. I stood in the
doorway to my office until the motion stopped (seemed to take forever!) and
then sat down and finished the report. But I guess I was just a little
distracted.

While the worm was a problem at that time, normal VMS security features, unless
disabled, would prevent it from ever getting very far today.

And finally, NO, I cannot provide a copy of the worm to anyone. The DOE CIAC
group at LLNL and the DOD CERT group at CMU should have it on file. Any release
would have to come from them. I don't think it likely they would release it
easily.

