Ok, some of these models look nice, but the low end is not very low.  In
fact, it's ridiculous. 

According the numbers in the November Inform, the cheapest way to get a
foot in the door for a VMS or OSF/1 workstation is: 

  what              $
  200 4/166     10295    (32 Mb, no CDROM, no monitor, 1 Gb disk)
  15" monitor     450
  CD-ROM          300    (estimate)
                11245   

That wouldn't have seemed so expensive in days of old, but hey, this is
really just a PCI based PC with a different processor.  One can easily buy
an equivalently equipped 486DX66 PC (although not from DEC), for around
3000. Allow another 1000 for all workstation licenses and installed
software, plus 1000 beyond that for the AXP chip and associated
engineering, and it still comes to less than half the price of the
workstation.  

Most of the following prices are straight out of catalogs and ads, but of
course I had to estimate a bit for the Motherboard and OS licenses. Note
that the ONLY engineering specific to this machine is in the design of the
Motherboard and possibly in writing a driver for the graphics card. Adding
it up this way, here's what the bottom end should cost: 

  what                     $      Commodity?
  32 Mb X $50/Mb        1600      Y
  PCI Motherboard+AXP   1600      Y (at least the chip is, according to DEC)
  (built in SCSI, ethernet)
  Power supply + case    300      Y
  15" Monitor            500      Y
  VMS or OSF/1 license   600      N
   (single user with misc.)
  Graphics (PCI)         400      Y (needs VMS or OSF/1 driver)
  Total, diskless       5000

  1 Gb disk             1000      Y
  2X CD-ROM              300      Y
  Total, w/disks        6300

Perhaps somebody at Digital would like to explain to us why they can't,
or won't, meet this price point?

