In article <EJH.94Nov10084407@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov>,
Edward Hartnett <ejh@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov> wrote:
>
>Howdy all.
>
>We here distribute some floating point (IEEE) data to various
>users. We generate it on a cray and various workstations, and now we
>have a user who wants to read the data from a fortran program on a
>decstation. He complains that he can't do this because of the order
>that the bytes are stored in. Is there any hope that we can help him
>solve his problem?
>--
>Edward Hartnett                         ejh@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov
> (301) 286-2396                          fax: (301) 286-1754
>
>"Tut! Tut!" cried Sherlock Holmes. "You must act, man, or you are
>lost. Nothing but energy can save you. This is no time for dispair." 

In C, you can use the swab() function to swap bytes ... exchanges
adjacent even and odd bytes in a given array.  It's easy enough
to write such a routine in fortran, something like ...

	SUBROUTINE SWAB( A, NBYTES )
	REAL*4  A(*)
	INTEGER NBYTES
C
	REAL*4  NOTVAX, VAX
	CHAR*1	SWAPIN(4), SWAPOU(4)
	EQUIVALENCE (NOTVAX, SWAPIN)
	EQUIVALENCE (VAX,    SWAPOU)
C
C	"NBYTES" must be even
C
	IF (MOD(NBYTES,2) .EQ. 0) THEN
		N = NBYTES
	ELSE
		N = NBYTES - 1
	ENDIF
C
	DO 10 I = 1, N
		NOTVAX = A(I)
		SWAPOU(1) = SWAPIN(2)
		SWAPOU(2) = SWAPIN(1)
		SWAPOU(3) = SWAPIN(4)
		SWAPOU(4) = SWAPIN(3)
		A(I) = VAX
10	CONTINUE
	RETURN
	END

