of the user stack.

On other systems, this address is defined in the macro USRSTACK.  For
example, on the Sun4s (SunOS 4.x), it's defined as

#define USRSTACK        0xF0000000

On the system I'm using (from uname - OSF1 bart V1.3 111 alpha), this
definition exists but is defined as follows:

#define       USRSTACK        ((vm_offset_t)u.u_stack_end)

where u presumably is of type struct user.

My question(s),

1.	Does this mean that the starting address of the stack can only be
	determined at run-time (unlike in SunOS)?  I mean, is there another
	way of determining this starting address value other than USRSTACK?

