	my needs), does this mean that only processes that have access to
	/vmunix and /dev/kmem can know this?  I'm assuming
	here that the process has to explicitly read /dev/kmem to get it's 
	uarea (where u_stack_end is defined).

3.	This is just a side question, was this done this way because they
	expect the starting stack address to be different for different
	processes?  If not, why did they define it as variable (even for
	a specific version of the kernel)?


Anyway, are these assumptions correct?  If not, then is there another
way to get the starting stack address?  Any suggestion would be appreciated.

