[Earlier I posted some press releases about Digital's new Multia desktop
system.  Attached is a description which gives further insight into the
concepts behind Multia, written by Joe Bates of Digital's Components and
Peripherals division.  If you have further questions about Multia you can
send them by e-mail to multia@digital.com. (I haven't seen a Multia yet,
but am looking forward to the opportunity.) - Steve]

Multia is _NOT_ just another Alpha workstation. That is not how it is being
sold, marketed or configured.

While Multia inside in fact does run WNT 3.5, it has software on top which gives
an easy-to-use and easily controllable (from a security/management perspective)
end-user environment. In effect, the end-user never really sees the bare NT OS
at all (unless the workgroup manager gives users access to it, which defeats the
idea of what Multia is all about).

This same software allows a network systems administrator the ability to manage
all the Multias on the network with relative ease through a windows-based
interface from a Multia management station (A Multia with the administrator
software installed). For example, it allows the manager to put end-users into
groups which can be managed as a single domain. The manager can control things
such as which clients have access to which servers, or which groups have access
to which applications. It also allows the administrator to install and update
applications and the operating environment. It is this management interface
which results in the Multia cost of ownership being lower when compareed to many
other desktop devices (studies are forthcoming).

One way that Multia takes advantage of WNT is by allowing the end-user Multia
stations to run multiple transports simultaneously. The net benefit is that this
makes it a great multiclient desktop - able to connect to multiple network
services from many different environments.

From an X-server perspective, Multia has X-server software which is highly
optimized for the Alpha chip inside. The result is that the X-windows graphics
performance on Multia is as fast as the high end X-windows terminals
(performance far in excess of the best Windows X-windows emulation software on
the fastest Intel PC's).

X-windows terminals can't run PC applications. PC's don't offer the X-windows
emulation graphic performance that a real Xwindow terminal is capable of
driving. Multia is a new category of desktop device. Just as a minivan is not
quite a van and not quite a station wagon, Multia is not quite a PC, not quite a
workstation and not quite an X-windows terminal.  Multia is a new and unique way
of integrating these devices/technologies and others into a package that's
very different from any other offering and very useful for the target
customer.

Today's corporate computing environment is evolving into a true client/server
environment with plenty of legacy applications mixed-in. If one were to sit back
and design what the ultimate client for the average end-user and MIS manager
in this environment might look like, it would look like Multia.

Multia can be configured without a floppy and without SCSI. This combined with
the management software makes it an excellent desktop device for environments
where security is a concern or requirement.

Multia is standards based in both the hardware interconnects (such as SCSI)
and the software.

The Multia box is small and sexy. The best demo is to walk into a customer with
the Multia in a small canvas notebook bag. Plug it into one of the customers off
the shelf existing PC monitor's and PC keyboard's and let it rip.

Multia is brand new, and under WNT it's very extensible for the future.
Display Postscript, IBM 3270 emulation and just about any other functionality
that there's a sufficient number of paying customers for is not only feasible
but probable.

Multia is not intended and was not designed as a stand-alone end-user device.
It's not intended for use at home as a PC. It is not intended for use by someone
who needs and is capable of managing and using all the capability of
the PC on his desktop. It _is_ intended for companies that have lots of desktops
in a client/server environment, who have lots of end-users that need to
run a variety of different network and local applications simultaneously in an
easy-to-use and manage environment. (i.e. let me get my job done but don't
bother me with all the other techie stuff).

What really excites me about Multia is that it is something new, different and
visionary from Digital. We have lots of me too Alpha products - sure Digital has 
Alpha workstations and Alpha servers as does the competition. Finally we're
using the Alpha chip in a box thats different from what everyone else offers.
Something that serves a purpose that is useful to _many_ clients in the Fortune
500. Although, its not the chip that makes Multia special, it could just as
easily run on an Intel chip. But given that its built by Digital, and the speeds
Alpha is capable of delivering in this package, I think it's highly appropriate
that it was built using Alpha. There will be other competitors in this new
"MultiClient Desktop" space. But Digital is the first here and as a result we
have the lead in this emerging market.

Reaction to Multia from the press, industry analysts, customers and business
partners so far has been universally positive. An X-terminal industry newsletter
called this the most significant product Digital has today. Any customer who has
actually seen one presented and demonstrated has been enamored with it. Based on
the response so far, I just hope we can build them fast enough to meet the
demand we're already seeing.

Multia is a Digital difference and a Digital advantage.
