In article <EJH.94Nov10084407@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov>, ejh@larry.gsfc.nasa.gov 
(Edward Hartnett) writes:

|>We here distribute some floating point (IEEE) data to various
|>users. We generate it on a cray and various workstations, and now we
|>have a user who wants to read the data from a fortran program on a
|>decstation. He complains that he can't do this because of the order
|>that the bytes are stored in. Is there any hope that we can help him
|>solve his problem?

Simplicity itself, if he's using a reasonably current version of DEC Fortran.
Have him add CONVERT='BIG_ENDIAN' to the OPEN statement for the unit
in question - this will cause DEC Fortran to do the byte swapping
during unformatted transfers of scalars and arrays (but not RECORDs)
automatically.  An alternative is to define the environment variable
FORT_CONVERTn , where n is the unit number, to BIG_ENDIAN before running
the program.

This feature is available in DEC Fortran for all supported platforms
(RISC ULTRIX, DEC OSF/1 AXP, OpenVMS AXP, OpenVMS VAX, Windows NT AXP).
Details vary on the "environment variable" method - see the User Manual
for further details.

