> Multia is not intended and was not designed as a stand-alone end-user device.
> It's not intended for use at home as a PC. It is not intended for use by someone
> who needs and is capable of managing and using all the capability of
> the PC on his desktop.

Fine, but CAN it be used at home as a PC by someone who needs and is capable of
managing and using all the capabilities of the PC? In other words, can one
throw away the Multia software and just use the box as a normal AXP WNT system?
In an ideal world, I wouldn't have to ask that question. But Multia seems to be
the only way you can get a usable AXP PC under $5k.

> What really excites me about Multia is that it is something new, different and
> visionary from Digital. We have lots of me too Alpha products - sure Digital has 
> Alpha workstations and Alpha servers as does the competition.

Well, it wouldn't occur to me to view Alpha workstations as "me too" products.
There aren't that many competitors selling Alphas. At least not outside the US.
The Alpha is a DEC difference and a DEC advantage. It would be even more of a
DEC advantage if the PC systems were priced more reasonably, and indeed the
Multia systems show just how high DEC's costs for the basic AlphaStation really
are.

> Finally we're
> using the Alpha chip in a box thats different from what everyone else offers.

Yeah, and if you want to know my reaction to the announcement, it was "Gee,
when will DEC sell people what they actually want to buy?" Multia is a piece of
software. The visionary innovation is the software, not the box it runs on. Now
if I were in charge of a bunch of non-technical users who need something like
Multia, I'd be looking for a piece of software I can install on the PCs the
users already have. Any reasonable PC can run this stuff with good performance,
except for X-Windows. The users who do need high performance X-Windows access
would get P90s or AXP PCs to run the software on, others would keep using their
PCs. There are shops that don't need X-Windows at all, or where it is used very
infrequently, or by a very small group of users. So, using existing hardware
would save money. Having to replace perfectly functional PCs with $3500 boxes
makes Multia that much more attractive. For $3500 I can buy a top notch
terminal emulator, an X-Windows emulator, a 3270 emulator, Linkworks and what
have you, and still have money left for a nice vacation at my favourite spot.
One nice vacation per user, that is. Now, THAT is something that would make me
popular with the users :-) If I didn't have anything, I might look at Multia
and find that it is the best alternative (I still think it's too expensive
compared to a 486/66 - I guess it would depend on whether I needed high
performance X-Windows support). But with an existing Intel base, Multia
wouldn't stand a chance. Now, if instead of looking for yet another ploy to
sell AXP chips, DEC had actually tried to sell people what they need, Multia
might be a software package available for, say, $750/user, and then it would
beat any combination of commercial terminal emulator/X-Windows emulator/3270
emulator and I'd be applauding with both hands and feet. It could still be
available for AXP and DEC could still sell turnkey boxes with everything
included. But it would appeal to a wider base and it wouldn't look
"proprietary". Yes, proprietary. You can wave all the open system stuff you
want, when the managers see that it only runs on Alpha chips, they'll think
"proprietary". Conversely, something that only runs on Intel chips is "open".
Go figure. But, like it or not, that's the way it is.

> An X-terminal industry newsletter
> called this the most significant product Digital has today.

Gee, now THAT surprises me. What were the contenders? "Me too" X-terminals? "Me
too" PC X-Windows emulators? Multia appeals to X bigots because it brings X to
the desktops of people who had absolutely no use for it but bought a Multia box
for the other functions. Then they can claim more X users than they really
have, and use it as an argument to talk more people into migrating to X.

> Any customer who has
> actually seen one presented and demonstrated has been enamored with it.

It's reassuring to see that the old DEC mentality is still alive and kicking
:-)

  Eric


