In article <1994Nov6.082056.20293@lugb.latrobe.edu.au>, cchd@lucifer.latrobe.edu.au (Huw Davies) writes:
> I'm trying to move a KDB50 from a 6310 to an 8800. I have moved the two
> boards into the BI on the 8800, connected them together with the
> ribbon cables, and set the BI node address. It is this last bit I'm not sure
> about (I've never played BI before - just Unibus/Qbus). I assume that
> only one board requires addressing - is this correct, and if so, which
> board?

All of the BI backplanes I've seen have had one address plug per slot. That's
the little blue thingie that goes in the back of the backplane with a number on
it.

Putting an address jumper in for the slot that doesn't need one is harmless;
if you've got a spare plug you may as well stick it in.

One of the boards will have the standard "BI corner" on it. You will see a large
PGA with a cooling tower on it near the corner of the board. There will be
at least one smaller DIP chip next to it. There may even be a TTL oscillator
in the corner. Here's where to look for the BI corner, assuming you're looking
at the side of the board with all the parts on it:


   +--------------------------+
   | ======================== | <- The four rows of square pads that connect
   |   +----+                 |    to the backplane
   |   |BIIC|                 |
   |   +----+                 |
   (| <- LED                  |
   |                          |
   |              -           |
   +--------------V-----------+
                   \ 'Nother LED

The board with the BIIC needs a node address. The other board doesn't.

----------------+------------------------------------------------------
