Well, my first reaction when I saw this was "Ah, it looks like there is finally
a way to get an AlphaStation for a price not altogether unrelated to the
manufacturing price. Looks like they finally managed to fool the managers into
not seeing that this would be more cost effective than the 3000 series". But,
after reading the details, I'm not sure any more. Four models are described,
two with and two without floppies. Other than that the descriptions are
identical, but the prices are different. And since when do floppies cost $150
in volume? :-) So, I'm curious as to the kind of hardware you get, *exactly*,
when you buy one of these Multia things. I don't have the slighest interest in
the Multia software, I just want an AXP PC. I don't think a box that is
essentially a P90 DECpc with an AXP daughterboard (with a *cheaper* processor)
ought to cost $9500. I even less think that the VMS version, with the CD-ROM
removed, ought to cost $1000 extra when DECdirect told me that I could buy an
OpenVMS license for DECpc AXP 150 or 3000-series box for some $450 (in Sweden,
so probably $300 in the US), which is about the same order as a retail NT
license.

I am really getting fed up with the way DEC is ruining its business
opportunities (and indirectly ruining other people's) with this ridiculous
old-iron pricing. DEC's PCs are competitively priced, perhaps not the cheapest,
but certainly not outrageously overpriced. A fully configured, high-end P90
system can be had for $4000. There's no reason why replacing the P90 with an
AXP 166 should increase the price tag by $5500-6500. The reason it does so is
that some manager figured it would be a bummer if any kid down the block could
buy a fully functional system for around $5000 which would run NT, VMS or
OSF/1, would perform significantly better than a $10k 3000-300X, and would be
within 10% of a $22k 3000-600. Imagine the disaster, people might actually want
to buy such a system. Stock might go up and wonderful investment opportunities
would be shattered.

The bottom line is that we have ordered two P90 systems in the last 2 weeks,
and we still don't have any AXP PC. No surprise - we can get nearly 3 of the
P90s for the price of a single AlphaStation 166. The P90s will run NT 3.5. We
have a couple customers that want to run our NT software on AXP systems. Well,
there's no secret. This is going to cost us over $10k in hardware, and, due
again to the big difference in hardware cost, not many customers are interested
in AXP, so the $10k investment won't be for hundreds of customers, but for a
handful. So the customers will have to pay a 4-figure surcharge. This in turn
will make it even more attractive for them to stick to Intel. If DEC would sell
AlphaStations for $5000, it would be a totally different story. It doesn't
really help that the AXP processor is twice faster if the price tag is around 3
times higher. One year ago I thought AXP was finally going to get us rid of the
Intel architecture, but no, the hard reality is that AXP is not cost effective
for PC-type systems, and by a league. So long live Intel and may we still have
16-bit support in hardware by the end of the century. In the name of the
conventional, and the expanded, and the extended memory, amen.

Anyway, I would love to hear that we can buy a $3500 Multia thingie, add
another 8-16M of RAM and a 1G disk, and get an AlphaStation 166 for $5000. But
before we try that, I'd like some kind of assurance that the Multia boxes are
"real" computers and not specifically designed around the interface, and that
this will actually work, because we don't have any use for Multia itself.

  Eric


