In article <Cyrrz7.AIw@crash.cts.com>, xcalibur@crash.cts.com (Ken Brucker) writes:
> I'm attempting to install a DEC rz57 scsi drive in a PC and so far I've
> failed miserably.  It would seem that the drive is not spinning up when
> the system boots.

Since I've explained this in email to quite a few different persons, and
the question keeps popping up, I figured I'd post it this time...  :-)

Look at the electronics board on the disk, at the end where the SCSI
connector is, and you'll find a few pins all the way over on one side of
it that are bent ninety degrees so that they point the same way as the
connector pins do.  The outermost pair of these pins control automatic
spin-up. I don't off-hand recall whether the disk spins up when they are
jumpered or when they are not -- but that should be obvious!  :-)

No guarantees; it's not my fault if you burn your disk; it worked for me.

-tih


