We have a SCSI system composed of two Digital Equipment PDP-11/70 minis 
and a collection of drives.

Periodically, we have difficulty accessing the drives.  Boots fail and 
tasks are unable to be read from the drives when the computer tries to 
load them.  Our physical configuration os somewhat unique, from what I 
gather.

As I mentioned, we have two DEC PDP-11/70 hosts.  Each PDP has a CDU-722 
SCSI (by CMD Technology Inc.) installed and configured to drive its 
differential SCSI interface.

Coming from the header connector on each CDU-722 we have used 
twisted-pair ribbon for noise immunity.  This is a length of about 10 
feet, and connects to a panel with a 50-pin Centronics connector.

The two hosts are connected to a common differential SCSI bus.  They have 
different host IDs.  From the panel with the Centronics connector, one 
host has a round cable 10 feet in length, and the other has a cable 30 
feet in length.  Both cable meet at a box which houses an NCR ADP-10 
differential-to-single_ended adaptor board.

Since the hosts are the "ends" of the differential bus, both have their 
internal terminators installed.  We have also tried disabling their 
TERMPOWER and supplying the diff-bus from the NCR, as well as the 
reverse, where TERMPOWER in the CDU-722s (both) was enabled and the NCR's 
was disabled.  This last config was recommended by CMD.

On the single-ended side of the ADP-10, its TERMPOWER is enabled, and its 
internal terminators are removed in favor of a external pass-though 
type.  The single-ended side runs between the NCR, a mini-tower enclosure 
housing 2 1GIG Fujitsu drives, a slim-line case with one more 1.3GIG 
Fujitsu and a Sony 600Meg Magneto-optical drive.  Last device is 
terminated externally.  We have verified that internal terminators have 
been removed from all drives, except the Sony MO, which we were not able 
to see without doing more disassembly than we were willing to try.

The problems that we are having some and go sporadically.  We have 
upgraded cables, swapped CDU interfaces and tried various orderings of 
drives.

What I would like to find is a _consultant_. Someone who can come on site 
and do an end-to-end analysis of this system.  DEC is not set up for 
this, at least our local office.

We have looked at analysers from companies like Ancot, but we really 
don't want to purchase such an expensive tool without having some 
expertise with which to analyse its output.  After having spent so much 
time trying to pinpoint the problem by doing device swaps, we still have 
no concrete idea what is happening when troubles arise, and so no 
definite approach to solving them.

Has anyone else tried stringing together a system like this, or had 
problems which they managed to track down?  Equipment vendors we have 
talked to seem to be unaware of anyone who specializes in analysing 
problems on SCSI systems of this type.  Most seem to be oriented towards 
PC-style systems and their problems, suggesting that adding active 
terminators (which are on order, by the way) or better cables (ditto) 
will solve the trouble.  While they may, I would still like to see a 
report from someone capable of diagnosing our difficulty which says "This 
was wrong." in a concrete way as a solid first step to solving it once 
and for all, where our "solutions" only seem for clear the problem for 4 
to six months.

thanks in advance
Kevin Beauchamp


