In article <398bin$727@quartz.ucs.ualberta.ca>, kbeaucha@gpu.srv.ualberta.ca (Kevin Beauchamp) writes:
> We are running a late version of RSX-11M+.  CMD has been contacted 
> regarding the OS and versions to verify that the dual host system is 
> acceptable with their hardware and our system/OS combination.

  You don't state what they said 8-) I'd be leery of any system with multiple
initiators unless I had that *particular* configuration certified.

> Also note: Drive access problem during boot up can occur without _any_ 
> dual-host activity.  Periodically, we get a "hung" SCSI bus (our only 
> visual indication is a solid access light on any device in the chain) 
> even before the system tries to mount additional drives.

  A light on only one device, one device + a controller, or more than that?
In general, this means that the device with the light on is either waiting
for some event that isn't happening, or has crashed it's on-board microcode.

> We typically use 3 foot interconnect SCSI cables.  With one from NCR to 
> mini-tower, one from mini-tower to slim-line, and one from slim-line to 
> MO, we have 9 feet of external cable.  Adding an average of one foot of 
> internal cable per device past the NCR (a guess) gives a total length of 
> 12 feet.
> 
> We have some 18" interconnect cables (with ferrites) also on order.
> 
> This is long, but not excessive.  Trouble is, when we drop devices, to 
> shorten the total length, our difficulties do not lessen.

  With single-ended SCSI, the shorter the better. Also, use good cables - I use
only DEC cables because I've been burned too many times by "quality" SCSI 
cables that weren't. Even with all-DEC SCSI cables, I have a system with 3
hard disks, a CD-ROM, QIC-150, and DAT drive that wants an active terminator.

> Is there something inherently wrong with multiple devices on a SCSI bus?  
> This system was implemented because we wanted the dual-host access, 
> higher densities and lower costs (than the RP06 Maytag-drives we were 
> running) SCSI offered.

  There's nothing inherently wrong with multiple devices per bus. You can get
sub-optimal performance (consider some 10MB/sec fast SCSI devices on a bus
with an async-only 1.5MB/sec device - the latter will hog the bus and starve
the fast devices). You can also get failures due to compatibility problems
between different peripherals. For example, a device that generates SCSI bus
resets, or one that locks up when it sees a synchronous transfer to a *dif-
ferent* device on the bus. I've seen all of these and more.

  As far as I know, RSX only supports dual-host access on Massbus devices,
though I'm not an RSX expert. MSCP is considered inherently non-dual-port-
able by DEC. CMD does it because they're converting MSCP to SCSI, which can
have multiple initiators and each system thinks it's the only one talking
to the devices on the bus. I've heard of problems with the dual-host support
on the 7xx-series controllers before, though.

> As far as I know, there is no longer a mainstream for PDPs.  We are 
> committed to this system for some time to come, as it hosts our campus' 
> distributed control system.

  I wasn't talking about a mainstream as applied to PDP's - I was talking
about the SCSI bus and the equipment configuration. When you start describing
your SCSI bus to people and they all go "ain't never heard of that 'un before"
you should start wondering 8-).

  What I'd do would be to ditch the dual-port stuff, run single-ended (unless
you have differential devices), put all of the devices in a single enclosure
near the CPU, use DEC cables and an actve terminator on each system. That'll
fix it. You could experiment with various bits and pieces to see if you can
avoid doing the whole job - you might find the problem, you might not.

