In article <CyLtJE.734@irvine.com> jim@irvine.com (James Zarbock) writes:

>   Is there some sort of "32 bit compatibility mode" for the C compiler
>   that will make the C compiler make longs and pointers 4 bytes?

There is the -taso switch which causes the C compiler to tell the
linker to make all addresses only use 32 bits (31 bits actually). The
sizeof an int is still 4 and the sizeof a pointer and a long are still
8, but this should let most uses of int = * = long work.

In fact, programs may run quite reliably without -taso. I know one
case where a program didn't fail until the linking in of a particular
sharable object library caused malloc to start returning >32 bit
addresses.

In the long run, of course, liberal use of -std1 (ANSI C) and lint to
catch these and other assumptions is the way to go.
